  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 315 AM EDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nBroad return flow is currently underway in the Great Lakes,\nbetween high pressure over New England, and an emerging cold\nfront in the central and northern plains. Warm/moist advection \nis interacting with a couple of shortwaves transiting the region\nearly this morning. This has resulted in a baroclinic leaf\npattern over and east of MI, along with sporadic light showers,\nespecially in eastern upper MI. However, the back edge of this\ncloud cover is beginning to advance east across northern Lake\nMI. \n\nWsw flow aloft persists into late Thursday, before a passing\nshortwave swings winds aloft to zonal and then wnw. The latter\nalso occurs as ridging in the northern US plains and Canadian\nprairie builds by this weekend.\n\nLeftover showers will depart, and cloud cover drastically\nreduce, over the next several hours. Mainly sunny skies are\nprogged for northern lower MI after mid-morning, while the\neastern UP has a bit more cloud cover today. Warm advection and\nat least partial sunshine will contribute to much warmer temps\ntoday. If anything, guidance has trended slightly warmer today.\nHighs today in northern lower MI range from the mid-upper 60s\nalong most coastlines, to near 70/lower 70s inland. The eastern\nUP will see low-mid 60s. Borrowing from the previous forecaster:\n\nOct 28 High Temperature Records\nLocation | High Temp (Year) \n     ANJ | 64 F (1924)\n     PLN | 71 F (1955)\n     GLR | 70 F (1955)\n     APN | 75 F (1989) \n     HTL | 73 F (1989) \n     TVC | 80 F (1927)\nWe should threaten ANJ/PLN/GLR and perhaps HTL.\n\nAs shortwaves race east across southern Canada, low pressure will\nconsolidate and strengthen over northern Ontario tonight. An\nassociated cold front enters the eastern UP and far northern\nlower MI late tonight, and the rest of northern MI Thursday.\nThough the strongest forcing remains in Canada, heights do\ngradually lower here beginning late tonight, and a (veering) \nlow level jet will support moisture transport northward in the \nwarm sector. Initial precip chances reach western Chip/Mack Cos,\nand the nw lower MI coast, toward midnight tonight. Likely to \ncategorical pops arrive in nw and n central lower MI, and the \neastern UP, overnight. Precip should become somewhat diffuse by \nthe time we reach sunrise, as the low level jet veers parallel \nto the incoming surface cold front. The nw half of the forecast \narea has thunder possible (noting the entire forecast area is in\nthe most recent day 1 outlook from SPC).\n\nSpotty showers remain possible Thu morning, but better moisture/\nforcing/instability will be suppressed south as the day proceeds.\nNo precip after mid-afternoon, and a likely period of at least \npartial sunshine, perhaps more than that in northern lower. This\nwill support continued mild temps, though not as warm as today \nexcept near Saginaw Bay. \n\nHigh pressure builds from the Canadian high prairie to the Great\nLakes, providing quiet wx to close the work week and even into\nthe weekend. For now, guidance is continuing to reduce pops this\nweekend, as relatively meager return flow gets gobbled up by\npersistent dry low-level air off of the high pressure, by then\nto our east. Precip chances are now progged to ramp up after\nMonday, as both northern and southern stream upper trofs advance\nfrom the west. Above-normal temps thus seem likely to persist \nwell into next week.",
